          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Pythagoras</strong><br>\nPythagoras was born on the island of Samos, Greece around 570 BC. His early life is shrouded in mystery, but he is thought to have been well educated, learning to play the lyre, write poetry, and recite Homer. Little is known about his family, but his influence on mathematics, philosophy, and music suggests he came from a background of significant learning and culture.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pythagoras's Travels and Learning</strong><br>\nHe traveled widely in his youth, visiting Egypt and possibly Babylon, to study under different teachers. It is during these travels that he learned about a wide range of subjects, including astronomy, geometry, and the belief systems of various cultures. These experiences significantly shaped his own philosophical and mathematical theories.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Founding of the Pythagorean Brotherhood</strong><br>\nAfter his travels, Pythagoras settled in Croton, in what is now southern Italy. Around 530 BC, he founded a philosophical and religious school that became known as the Pythagorean Brotherhood. Members of the brotherhood followed a strict lifestyle that included a shared belief system, common property, and adherence to various rules.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
